{
  "comment": "PLAN-38 (section 2): least-privilege RabbitMQ users, one per service with a bus, derived from the live inventory reports/plan38-rabbit-topology.json (and MassTransit 8.5.10 naming). No secrets: passwordEnv names the .env variable. hrm-bootstrap step 6 creates the users and sets their passwords and permissions on every deploy. configure = the service's endpoint prefix (ResolveEndpointPrefix: the .Web assembly name, kebab-case, plus '-'), the shared message-type exchanges, MassTransit's fault exchanges, and the bus temporary queue of MassTransit (machine_process_bus_id, no hyphen, so it can never match a prefixed name). read = the same expression as configure (review F2: consuming, getting or purging is limited to the service's own queues, and binding to the shared exchanges it declares). write = .* (publishing to shared exchanges and sending to another service's endpoint stay open; the boundary between services is held by configure and read). Residual: the bus temporary-queue pattern matches every service's temporary queue (no request client is in use). The gateway has no bus and no user.",
  "vhost": "/",
  "users": [
    {
      "service": "hr-core-web",
      "endpointPrefix": "hr-core-web",
      "user": "mq_hr_core",
      "passwordEnv": "RABBITMQ_HR_CORE_PASS",
      "permissions": { "configure": "^(hr-core-web-.*|HRManagement\\.MessageContracts\\.(Events|Commands):.*|MassTransit:Fault.*|[^-]+_bus_[^-]+)$", "write": ".*", "read": "^(hr-core-web-.*|HRManagement\\.MessageContracts\\.(Events|Commands):.*|MassTransit:Fault.*|[^-]+_bus_[^-]+)$" }
    },
    {
      "service": "hr-contracts-web",
